#include <iostream>

int digitSum(int n)
{
    int total{};

    while (n > 0)
    {
        total += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }

    return total;
}

int main()
{
    int x{};

    while (std::cin >> x)
    {
        std::cout << x << ' ' << digitSum(x) << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

explanation:
## The program

```cpp
int digitSum(int n)
{
    int total{};

    while (n > 0)
    {
        total += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }

    return total;
}

int main()
{
    int x{};
    while (std::cin >> x)
        std::cout << x << ' ' << digitSum(x) << "\n";
}
```

## The parameter is a copy

This is the part worth being sure about. `digitSum` divides `n` down
to zero, and yet:

```cpp
int x{ 123 };
std::cout << digitSum(x);   // 6
std::cout << x;             // 123, untouched
```

C++ passes by value: calling the function copies the argument into
the parameter, and the parameter is a local variable of the
function like any other. Destroying it costs the caller nothing.

That is why the caller can print `x` and `digitSum(x)` on the same
line without saving a copy first — and why the "take a backup before
calling" habit people bring from other languages is unnecessary
here. It is also why `void addOne(int n) { ++n; }` does nothing at
all, which is the subject of the exercises on references.

## Declare before use

C++ reads a file top to bottom, and `main` cannot call a name it has
not seen. Either define the function above `main`, as here, or
declare it above and define it below:

```cpp
int digitSum(int n);        // declaration: the name and the shape

int main() { /* ... */ }

int digitSum(int n)         // definition: the body
{
    /* ... */
}
```

The second form is what headers are for, and it is the reason a
header file can describe a library without containing it.

## Zero, again

`digitSum(0)` returns 0 because the loop never runs and `total`
started at its identity. The same shape as the digit-reversal
exercise, and the same reason it needs no special case.

## What this does not check

Negative numbers. `digitSum(-123)` returns 0 here, because `while (n
> 0)` is false immediately — quietly wrong rather than an error. A
version that meant to handle them would take the absolute value
first and would have to decide what `digitSum(INT_MIN)` means, since
that value has no positive counterpart.
